# Generated dashboard artefacts.
#
# These four are pure outputs of scripts/build-all.js. Nobody hand-edits them,
# but every branch that publishes a test run rewrites them — so two branches
# touching unrelated projects still collide here (run counts, pass rates, the
# "Generated" timestamp). Refereeing that conflict by hand is meaningless.
#
# `merge=ours` keeps our side instead of halting the merge. That is only correct
# because the file is regenerated immediately afterwards — see the post-merge
# hook in .githooks/. Without the regeneration step this WOULD silently drop the
# other side's projects from the landing page.
#
# Requires (once per clone, both are local config and are not carried by clone):
#   git config merge.ours.driver true
#   git config core.hooksPath .githooks
# Without the driver, git ignores the attribute and you get a normal conflict —
# harmless, just unhelpful. Run `npm run build` and take the regenerated file.
index.html  merge=ours
hub.json    merge=ours
hub.js      merge=ours
projects/*/index.html  merge=ours
projects/*/data.json   merge=ours
projects/*/data.js     merge=ours
